# Round 5 — findings

## Question A — can conversion split a list around embedded blocks on its own?

**Yes.** `runner/split.mjs` splits a `<ul>`/`<ol>` around any embedded `<pre>` or
`<img>`/`<figure>`/`<picture>`/`<svg>`, at any nesting depth, into list parts (own
`<ol>`/`<ul>` clone, ordered `start` resumed correctly) and bare code/image units, in
document order, with no whole-item duplication. Verified on 8 fixtures: pre in a
top-level item, pre nested 2 and 3 levels, image nested, two blocks in one list, a block
first in one item and last in another, text before-and-after a block in one item, and
`start=5`. Full data in `results/round-5-cases.json`, write-up in `results/round-5.md`.

**The empty-item question resolves to "both, and that's fine."** A resumed part's leading
stub renders, in the raw markdown text, as the bare ordinal glued to the nested list's own
first marker on the same line (`2.  3.  Item B.3`) — it looks collapsed by eye. It is valid
CommonMark anyway (the same rule that makes `1. 2. foo` legal compact nesting), and
`markdown-it` (same config as `spoken/to_spoken.py`) recovers the intended structure every
time: a real, empty `<li>` whose only child is the resumed nested list. Confirmed for all
three fixtures that produce this shape (ordered 2-deep, ordered 3-deep, unordered).
Caveat: this is a CommonMark-compliance fact, not guaranteed for a non-CommonMark
renderer.

**Unprompted discovery:** `to_spoken.py`'s items-mode already drops an empty item's own
line entirely (its text is `""`, so `walk()` never appends a line for it, though the
running ordered counter still advances past it) — there is never actually a spoken bare
"2." today. Audio A/B in `audio/LISTEN.md`'s new section tests a hand-built "kept" variant
(with the ordinal put back) against that real "removed" behavior for all three cases;
6 files, `audio/r5-<case>-<kept|removed>.ogg`. Both ordered cases show a real duration gap
(kept is 1.25s / 2.5s longer) — something is being voiced for the bare ordinal, unheard by
me. The bullet case shows **zero** duration difference — Kokoro's `split_pattern='\n+'`
(round 4's finding) swallows a leading blank line before synthesis ever starts.

A bug surfaced building this: a whitespace-only text node left after pulling a nested list
out of an item was being flushed as a spurious empty `<li>`. Fixed in `split.mjs` by having
the item-content flush discard whitespace-only buffers; the fix and the reason for it are
a comment in the file, since real HTML will hit this same case.

## Question B — what would runtime-synthesized multi-unit splitting break?

Facts only, from `pipeline/src/runtime/{units,recipe,paths,validate}.ts` and
`injection/entry.ts`. No splitting exists in the runtime today; this is about a
hypothetical runtime-side split, as opposed to round 4's recipe-side
`splitListAroundCode` (`recipes/r4.ts:83-120`).

- **Paths.** `AnnotatedUnit.path` (`units.ts:21-27`) is one `pathOf(element)`
  (`paths.ts:15-32`) per unit, off one real DOM element (`entry.ts:91,104,114`). `pathOf`
  requires an unbroken `.parent` chain of tag nodes up to `<html>`; `resolvePath`
  (`paths.ts:34-55`) walks back down that chain by sibling position in a **freshly
  re-parsed** DOM (`validate.ts:189`). A synthesized list-part clone was never part of the
  parsed article tree, so it has no such chain and no path `resolvePath` could ever
  recover. The carved-out code/image sub-units are the easy half: the real `<pre>`/`<img>`
  node already sits at a real position, so `pathOf` works on it unmodified.

- **The nesting check** (`resolveUnits`, `validate.ts:18-45`) flags any pair of units
  whose paths are ancestor/descendant of each other, **either direction**
  (`validate.ts:34-39`). Keeping the original whole `<ol>` as one unit's path while also
  emitting a separate unit for the `<pre>`/`<img>` inside it trips this immediately — the
  two paths nest. A working split needs every unit's path to be a real, disjoint sibling
  position, which detached synthesized clones cannot express at all.

- **Inventory** (`checkInventory`, `validate.ts:111-152`) and **leftover-text**
  (`checkLeftoverText`, `validate.ts:154-185`) both key off `resolved.elements` — real,
  disjoint elements with descendant coverage would satisfy both; neither adds a
  requirement beyond what the nesting check already forces.

- **Image unit fields** (`units.ts:6-17`, `entry.ts:95-106`): `src`/`alt` plus a non-null
  `element` for `pathOf` — fine for a runtime-synthesized image unit, since it hands back
  a real `<img>`/`<svg>` node.

- **The boundary shape** (`toBoundaryUnit`, `units.ts:29-34`) strips `path` and is
  unaffected either way; every constraint sits upstream of it, in `pathOf`/`resolveUnits`.

